  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nQuiet and dry weather expected through Thanksgiving morning. It\nwill be much cooler, with highs mainly in the lower 30s to \nlower 40s during the afternoons. We could see widespread lows in\nthe teens Wednesday and Thursday mornings which would be the \ncoldest of the season thus far. Will then see deteriorating \nconditions Thanksgiving afternoon with the brunt of the impacts \novernight into Black Friday morning.\n\nThere is good agreement that as the upper level low moves \nnorthward into the eastern Great Lakes, a secondary surface low \nwill develop along a warm front near Long Island NY Thursday \nnight, then move northeastward and right overhead by Friday \nmorning. There will be rather strong dynamics with a jet streak \nleft exit region adding some forcing for ascent.\n\nLooking most likely there will be one south-north moving heavy \nband of rain impacting the region sometime Thursday night-Friday\nmorning, where a quick 1.0-1.5 inches of rain is looking \nlikely. Some wrap- around showers are likely to linger into \nFriday and even the weekend as the upper-level low takes its \ntime clearing the region, keeping us in cyclonic westerly flow \nas it does so. Expect a somewhat showery and unsettled start to \nthe weekend, but additional precipitation should be light and \nbrief compared to the primary storm system.\n\nNot expecting snowfall to be a player with the initial thump of\nmoisture Thursday night as 850 mb temperatures will be hovering\naround +3 to +6 C. However there has been a persistent cold air\ndamming signal as the antecedent airmass will be quite cold. \nThe concern would be some of this near-surface cold air would \nget jammed up against the mountains, and with milder air aloft, \nthere could be a potential freezing rain for the interior. Will\nneed to watch this closely over the next day or two. Chances \nfor snow will return by Friday afternoon as the airmass aloft \ncools behind the cold front, initially for the mountains, but \nsome flakes for the lower elevations may be possible by Friday \nnight-Saturday.\n\nAs for wind, expecting a quick burst with the heavy rain \nThursday night-early Friday. Guidance is indicating 60-80 kts at\n850 mb sweeping over the region from south to north. Strong \nwinds along the coast may result in some isolated tree \ndamage. Not much fanfare expected in the interior given the \ninversion/CAD signal there.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThis month's spring tides occur this week, bringing greater\ntidal swings along the coast. High water is expected during the\nlate morning semidiurnal tides today through Thursday. With 1-2\nft waves tonight through Thanksgiving morning, coastal flooding\nis not expected through this period. \n\nThe tide cycle in focus for coastal flooding potential is the \nFriday high tide around noon. Easterly wind fetch will have \nbeen underway around 24 hours as primary low consolidates \ninland. This fetch will be the primary component to coastal \nsurge as the storm's inland slide limits additional pressure \ninfluences. Wind speeds over the Gulf of Maine gradually exceed \nGale force Thursday night through Friday morning, driving a wind\nwave that could see nearshore wave heights sharply climb to \naround 12 ft Friday morning. Combining tide, surge, and wave \naction, Minor coastal flooding will be possible with splash-over\nalong several roads from the NH Seacoast through Casco Bay. \n\nWhile wind direction and magnitude is of greater confidence \nleading into Friday morning, lower confidence is assigned to \ntiming and progression of a wind shift later in the morning that\ncould limit wave heights and surge overlapping with high tide.\nGreater certainty will come once ensembles better resolve any\npossible cyclogenesis along the ME coast.",
